WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:09.827
Picture your small lunch kitchen landing its first big customer: an office that wants two hundred boxes a day.

2
00:00:09.827 --> 00:00:12.923
On day one, their buyer opens a box to check it.

3
00:00:13.067 --> 00:00:17.424
Next to the lunch she finds a kitchen knife, and a half-empty bag of flour.

4
00:00:17.424 --> 00:00:20.504
And taped under the lid is the key to your back door.

5
00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:22.456
Nobody packed those on purpose.

6
00:00:22.456 --> 00:00:27.248
They were lying on the counter while the lunch was made, so they went into the box.

7
00:00:27.248 --> 00:00:29.327
And the lunch itself tastes perfect.

8
00:00:29.467 --> 00:00:30.622
That is the trap.

9
00:00:30.622 --> 00:00:36.328
An image fills up the same way, and the app inside works perfectly, so nobody looks.

10
00:00:36.867 --> 00:00:40.466
Our hello-devops image from before has exactly these three.

11
00:00:40.466 --> 00:00:44.859
It was built in one stage, so everything used to build it ships with it.

12
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.696
The knife is pip, the tool that installed the app's packages.

13
00:00:48.696 --> 00:00:51.848
The app never needs it again, but an attacker could.

14
00:00:51.848 --> 00:00:54.817
The flour is whatever else the build left behind.

15
00:00:54.817 --> 00:00:59.725
And the key is a secret token, left where anyone who pulls the image can read it.

16
00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:04.152
Wouldn't it be nice to cook in the kitchen, and ship only the lunch?

17
00:01:04.152 --> 00:01:06.723
Leave the knife and the flour on the counter.

18
00:01:06.867 --> 00:01:08.706
That is a multi-stage build.

19
00:01:08.706 --> 00:01:11.070
One Dockerfile, with two FROM lines.

20
00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:14.223
Each FROM starts a new stage, from a clean base.

21
00:01:14.223 --> 00:01:16.259
The first stage is the kitchen.

22
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:22.900
In the kitchen we make a virtual environment, a folder called venv that holds the app's Python packages.

23
00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:26.525
pip installs them into it, and then we remove pip from it.

24
00:01:26.667 --> 00:01:30.280
The second stage starts again from a fresh, empty base.

25
00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:32.973
It even removes the base image's own pip.

26
00:01:33.133 --> 00:01:40.347
Then it reaches back into the kitchen for exactly one thing: the venv, with every package already installed.

27
00:01:40.347 --> 00:01:41.616
And it adds app.py.

28
00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:44.147
Only the last stage becomes the image.

29
00:01:44.147 --> 00:01:48.533
The kitchen, with its knife and flour, stays behind in the build cache.

30
00:01:49.133 --> 00:01:51.615
So how much smaller is the image now?

31
00:01:51.615 --> 00:01:52.688
Pause and guess.

32
00:01:54.867 --> 00:01:55.650
Barely.

33
00:01:55.650 --> 00:02:00.683
The content went from 51.8 megabytes to 49.6.

34
00:02:00.867 --> 00:02:02.896
Well, that is not much of a diet.

35
00:02:02.896 --> 00:02:06.277
And that's fine, because size was never the point here.

36
00:02:06.467 --> 00:02:07.998
The point is what left.

37
00:02:07.998 --> 00:02:14.191
A vulnerability scanner called Trivy checks every package against public lists of known bugs.

38
00:02:14.191 --> 00:02:16.788
Removing pip took away 5 high findings.

39
00:02:16.788 --> 00:02:20.317
They sat in libraries that pip carries inside itself.

40
00:02:20.467 --> 00:02:26.717
The 44 that stayed come from the Debian base, and on the day we scanned, none of them had a fix yet.

41
00:02:26.717 --> 00:02:28.843
You rebuild when Debian ships one.

42
00:02:28.843 --> 00:02:33.031
Your numbers will differ, because new bugs are published every day.

43
00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:35.401
Next, who opens the box.

44
00:02:35.401 --> 00:02:37.426
The old image runs as root.

45
00:02:37.426 --> 00:02:39.977
Ask it who am I, and it says root.

46
00:02:40.133 --> 00:02:45.598
The walls of a container fence it in, but it is still root on a kernel it shares with everyone.

47
00:02:45.598 --> 00:02:49.107
Break out through one bug, and you arrive with root's rights.

48
00:02:49.267 --> 00:02:52.062
Two lines in the final stage fix it.

49
00:02:52.062 --> 00:02:56.797
Create a normal user with the number 10001, and switch to it.

50
00:02:56.797 --> 00:02:59.825
Now the same question says: user 10001.

51
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.170
Write the number, not the name.

52
00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:06.580
A name means nothing until someone reads the image's user list.

53
00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:10.431
A number like 10001 is plainly not zero, which is root.

54
00:03:11.733 --> 00:03:13.620
Last, the key under the lid.

55
00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:19.144
Sometimes a build step needs a secret, like a token to download a private package.

56
00:03:19.144 --> 00:03:22.783
A token is a secret string that works like a password.

57
00:03:22.933 --> 00:03:25.668
The obvious way is a build argument.

58
00:03:25.668 --> 00:03:29.390
So we build with a fake token, s3cr3t demo token.

59
00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:34.403
The build finishes, and the token is not in the running container.

60
00:03:34.403 --> 00:03:35.618
Who can find it?

61
00:03:35.618 --> 00:03:36.833
Pause and guess.

62
00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:40.821
Anyone who pulls the image.

63
00:03:40.821 --> 00:03:45.271
docker history, the list of steps that built it, prints the token.

64
00:03:45.271 --> 00:03:45.676
Twice.

65
00:03:45.867 --> 00:03:47.956
The other obvious ways leak too.

66
00:03:47.956 --> 00:03:50.894
ENV stores the value in the image's settings.

67
00:03:50.894 --> 00:03:55.661
And copying a token file, then deleting it later, leaves it in its layer.

68
00:03:55.661 --> 00:03:56.901
The soup jar again.

69
00:03:57.067 --> 00:03:59.128
The safe way is a secret mount.

70
00:03:59.128 --> 00:04:06.509
The secret is handed to that one RUN step while it runs, and never lands in a layer, the cache, or the history.

71
00:04:06.509 --> 00:04:08.969
Search the history now: zero matches.

72
00:04:09.133 --> 00:04:10.618
So here is the rule.

73
00:04:10.618 --> 00:04:15.145
A secret in ARG, ENV, or a deleted file ships with the image.

74
00:04:15.145 --> 00:04:16.407
Mount it instead.

75
00:04:17.133 --> 00:04:18.421
A few honest costs.

76
00:04:18.421 --> 00:04:22.285
A smaller base, with no shell inside, is harder to debug.

77
00:04:22.285 --> 00:04:29.402
A base pinned to its exact fingerprint stops getting fixes by itself, so teams let a bot propose updates.

78
00:04:29.402 --> 00:04:34.824
And a token that ever went in as an ARG must be treated as leaked, and replaced.

79
00:04:35.533 --> 00:04:37.194
So the moral of the story.

80
00:04:37.194 --> 00:04:40.068
Cook in the kitchen, and ship only the lunch.

81
00:04:40.068 --> 00:04:41.983
Open the box as a normal user.

82
00:04:41.983 --> 00:04:44.346
And never tape the key under the lid.

83
00:04:44.346 --> 00:04:49.711
Our final image also carries a health check that tests the app every thirty seconds.

84
00:04:50.267 --> 00:04:51.124
Quick check.

85
00:04:51.124 --> 00:04:54.555
Last month, a token went in as a build argument.

86
00:04:54.555 --> 00:04:57.057
Today you switch to a secret mount.

87
00:04:57.057 --> 00:04:58.272
Are you safe now?

88
00:05:00.467 --> 00:05:00.673
No.

89
00:05:00.673 --> 00:05:04.865
Every image built last month still carries it in its history.

90
00:05:04.865 --> 00:05:07.271
Treat it as leaked, and replace it.
